<[Gary] GARY-S> do you feel comfortable responding to the question, doug
<[Gary] GARY-S> posted as you enter the RTC - do you prefer DO...LOOP or FOR... 
NEXT ? Why ?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> is here.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> DO...LOOP, its more general.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Both.
<[Dave] OLORIN> (grin)
<[Dave] OLORIN> I use DO ... LOOP because its what I'm used to.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Ah, historical precedant.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Or general laziness.
<[Dave] OLORIN> (grin)
<[Gary] GARY-S> what say ye, Wil !
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> FOR..NEXT better than DO..LOOP, but...
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> It's 18 years too late.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Well, its conceptually simpler anyway.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> What about defining the words which can be used to create 
those constructs?
<[Gary] GARY-S> Is syntax a consideration at x3/j14 ?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> 9 FOR ( ten times) NEXT is conceptually simpler?
<[Gary] GARY-S> FOR..NEXT syntax should make it the construct of choice...
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Wil, so 0 FOR NEXT iterates once, does -1 FOR NEXT iterate 
at all?
<[Gary] GARY-S> but I sure wouldn't want to trash 18 years of DO..LOOP code
<[Dave] OLORIN> Of course 0 0 DO ( lots) LOOP is also a pain.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Gary, Pygmy has FOR NEXT, with DO LOOP as add on 
compatability screens.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Doug, what do you want -1 FOR NEXT to do?
<[Gary] GARY-S> I am satisfied PYGMY is reflecting (forecasting) what will be 
the future..
<[Gary] GARY-S> ie: both will be available
<[Dave] OLORIN> Wil: Look at me funny.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Don't know, Wil.  I want a way for FOR NEXT to be able to 
execute 0 times.
<[Dave] OLORIN> (is still of the opinion that computers should have a "You must 
be crazy but I'll do it anyway" light)
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> If you can build DO LOOP on top of FOR NEXT, which is more 
'Forth' like?
<[Gary] GARY-S> FOR...NEXT will make one pass regardless
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> I've got a noisly line, BRB
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> disconnected.
<[Gary] GARY-S> Wil - current status of your Edu-Forth
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> I've been wondering if FPC or PYGMY might be better.
<D.PHILIPS2> is here.
<[Gary] GARY-S> You already know my vote if you're taking a straw poll
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> -1 FOR NEXT behaviour shud depend on hardware. ...
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Some hardware will do it once, other hardware Virtually 
Forever.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Wrong!
<[Dave] OLORIN> Language definitions should never mark something as "Hardware 
Dependent".
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> IOW, a standard shud leave it undefined.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> What about a looping construct like C's for(init;test;
repeat).   Syntax is open issue, of course.
<[Dave] OLORIN> You can mark it as "undefined", but making it...
<[Dave] OLORIN> ..."hardware dependent" makes it too quirky.
<[Dave] OLORIN> (Ahh. We agree)
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> How is 'hardware dependant' semantically different from 
'undefined'?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> "hw dep" is a form of "undef".
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Right.  What does ANS say about the definitions of these 
things?
<[Dave] OLORIN> Hardware Dep. implies that something may be used.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Undefined implies "Leave that sucker alone".
<[Dave] OLORIN> Slight difference in semantics.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> I wish I cud find the circa 1974 review of C where the C "for" 
is criticized.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> ACM Computing Reviews.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Did you agree with the review, Wil?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Dave, if you are interested in portability, "leave that sucker 
