<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Actually, the idea of getting the loop control info in one 
place seems a good one to me (of course, I'm a 'C' programmer by profes
sion :-).
<[Dave] OLORIN> <-- also a professional C programmer. Mostly.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> If it isn't marked as portable in the standard, don't use 
it, or if you
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> (My orthographic regulator is still broken; another early nite 
for me.)
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> must, hide it behind a word or two that can be 
reimplemented without tainting the rest of the program.
<[Dave] OLORIN> The standard should be portable. That's the point of having one.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Bye, Wil?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> When I first encountered the F83 "?DO" I thot it was great.  
Now I know it as a kludge.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Doug, Not yet.  I thot I'd forewarn.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> I'm still curious to here your objections to something like 
C's 'for'.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> I dont object to it; it's what I want.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Great!  Did you think the ACM criticism specious, or was 
there really room for improvement?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> In defensive programming there shud be a way to do something 
Zero Times gracefully.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> The ACM reviewer didnt know his ....
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Now in historical perspective it's amusing.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> What I want to say is "for( i = INITIAL; i < LIMIT; i += 
INCREMENT) something;
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> And how do you want to say that you should say that in 
Forth's syntax?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> There is no way to do that in with a DO loop or ?DO.
<[Dave] OLORIN> To do that in Forth you have two options. You can have a loop 
function take...
<[Dave] OLORIN> a word as its increment/whatever argument...
<[Dave] OLORIN> ...or you can include something along the lines of Lisps lambda 
functions,
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> But you can also say: for(i=head;i != NULL; i = i->next) 
something;
<[Dave] OLORIN> ...a function defined namelessly inside of a loop to be 
executed as the
<[Dave] OLORIN> ...pre-loop-test function.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Agreed. Forth's DO loops are too restrictive.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> I was briefly reviewing my smalltalk books today.  They 
have the
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> idea of iterating over the contents of containers, and the 
idea of
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> looping while condition is true/false.  I don't have them 
with me, so I
<[Dave] OLORIN> E.g. ( initial condition ) DO ( body ) (increment) {[ 
current-loop done? ]} LOOP
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> It works both ways: You can write ?DO ... incr +LOOP but it's 
a mess.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> ... in C.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Which is essentially a BEGIN WHILE REPEAT.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> can't remember how you iterate over integer ranges without 
hitting everything,
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> i.e. for(i=0; i< 100; i += 5) foo;
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> The Forth 83 LOOP is very strange.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> So, Wil, how do we get something like C's for with Forth's 
syntax?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Termination duz not depend on a condition but an action.
<[Gary] GARY-S> The idea was to fix 79's no-pass
<[Dave] OLORIN> Ah well. I'm off.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> As you said, BEGIN WHILE REPEAT.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> DAVE, wait.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Bye Dave.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Yes?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Dave, would you try your hand at ?DO incr +LOOP in C?
<[Dave] OLORIN> Sure.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Doing in C exactly what the Forth duz.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> OK, catch you later. Bye.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> I'd like to see your answers also.
