<[Dave] OLORIN>     if (counter != limit)
<[Dave] OLORIN>        while (counter != limit)
<[Dave] OLORIN>           {
<[Dave] OLORIN>              ....
<[Dave] OLORIN>              counter += increment;
<[Dave] OLORIN>           }
<[Dave] OLORIN>
<[Dave] OLORIN>
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Dave. No.
<[Dave] OLORIN> Like that? Or do I need to go hunt up my copy of the standard 
again.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> You have to account for counter passing limit but not 
equaling it.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Right. And the conditions depend on relative position in the 
"circle of numbers".
<[Dave] OLORIN> Ahh./exit
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> My copy of "Starting Forth" says:  'The boundary always 
lies between
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> limit and limit - 1, regardless of which direction +LOOP is 
