<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Wil, why the urge to write DO LOOP in C?  Does this mean 
that C's for is not fit for Forth because it isn't easily moldable into t
hat construct?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> No. This shows the kludginess of DO LOOP.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> I'll grant that.  What about doing a Forth syntax for C's 
for loop?
<[Gary] GARY-S> Bouma points to sloppy index handling as Forth's culprit - 
comments ?
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Part of it.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Forth 83 DO LOOP is a very complex, hard to understand, 
concept.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Gary, I started that thread, but you didn't get it because 
of 'ForthNet' in the header silliness!
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Mitch's "(unsigned) a>= (unsigne) b" may be a good approach.
<[Gary] GARY-S> I know - I see all the references - trouble maker :-)
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Forth syntax for C "for" loop was given already: BEGIN WHILE 
REPEAT.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Wil, but it must work for crossing the boundary the other 
way too, the test
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> depends on the direction of the loop.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Wil:  That doesn't cut it,  it still leaves the third 
clause separated from the other two.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> I want a syntax that keeps all three parts together.
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Oh, you want to get it all up front.
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> Yes, that is the point.  The beauty of the 'for' construct 
is that it
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> keeps all the relevant info in place!
<[Doug] D.PHILIPS2> in place -> in one place
<[Wil] W.BADEN1> Right.
